To avoid detection while running the browser's background script, you can follow these steps:

1. Use a Content Security Policy (CSP) header: The CSP is a security feature that allows you to specify which sources of content your web page should allow and block. You can add a `Content-Security-Policy` header to your HTML document or style sheet to restrict the execution of scripts from external domains.
   Example:
   ```html
   <meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy" content="default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://example.com; object-src 'self' https://example.com;">
   ```

2. Use a trusted source for scripts: Instead of loading scripts directly from external domains, use a trusted source such as a CDN (内容分发网络), a local file, or an iframe with a preloaded script tag.
   Example:
   ```html
   <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/jquery/3.6.0/jquery.min.js"></script>
   ```
   This ensures that the script is only executed from the same origin as the webpage, reducing the risk of cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks.

